Holden stands in the quiet halls of the museum, surrounded by ancient artifacts and towering dinosaur skeletons. The air is cool and still, a stark contrast to the chaos of the city outside. He walks slowly, his footsteps echoing softly.

HOLDEN
(to himself)
Everything here stays the same. No matter how many times you come back, it's always the same.

He stops in front of a diorama of Native American life, frozen in time. The figures are lifelike, their expressions serene. Holden feels a pang of longing.

HOLDEN
(softly)
I wish I could just stay here forever. No phoniness, no changes.

He sits on a bench, staring at the diorama. Memories of his childhood visits flood his mind—times when things felt simpler, when he felt more connected to the world.

HOLDEN
(whispering)
Maybe I just need to find my own diorama. Somewhere I can just... be.

A museum guard passes by, nodding politely. Holden nods back, feeling a rare sense of peace. He takes a deep breath, standing up to continue his journey through the museum.
